The fatigue bars were heat treated post-process in order to relieve the residual stress and homogenize the microstructure. This will allow consistent assessment of the as-built surface roughness. The bars were stress relieved at 1065 °C for 90 min and furnace cooled.
The bars were removed from the build plate using wire EDM and post-processed through the standard solution and aging heat treatment for AM Alloy 718 [24], [25]. No additional heat treatment was used to reduce the porosity because it has been shown that the surface roughness will dominate the fatigue failure [12].
2.2. Surface measurements
The entire surface of the gage section of every fatigue bar was measured using two nondestructive methods. SL scanning and CT were used to characterize the surface and calculate the surface roughness metrics. These methods were chosen because they can be performed on the material before fatigue testing and can capture the entire surface, which ensures that all surface features are captured. The chosen metrics used to quantify areal surface roughness measurements, designated with S, are defined and parameterized in ISO 25178 [26]. The surface is measured in the x-y plane with z representing the surface height. The heights are stored in a matrix z(x, y). The mean plane is from all measured points on the surface, and the surface metrics are calculated from the height variations from this plane. For both methods, Sa (arithmetic mean height) and Sv (maximum pit height) were calculated using Eqs. (1), (2) respectively, where A is the area of the surface and z(x, y) is the matrix of surface heights. Both surface roughness metrics are related to the contour process parameters used in fabrication. Sa and Sv are chosen for this investigation because these metrics are typically reported in relation to fatigue life when considering traditional manufacturing processes and have been shown to relate to the contour processing parameters [8].
